// ============================================================================
//  RoamWise - Firestore Security Rules   (STABLE BUILD v15.10 / vc79 - 2026-08-29)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  HOW TO DEPLOY (2 minutes, do this whenever rules change):
//    1. Firebase Console  ->  your project  ->  Build  ->  Firestore Database
//    2. Click the  "Rules"  tab at the top
//    3. Select all existing text, delete it, paste THIS ENTIRE FILE
//    4. Click  "Publish"
//    5. Update the  meta/rulesVersion  Firestore document (the `version`
//       field, see the match block below) to match the version marker in
//       this header (e.g. "v15.10") - the app's staleness probe reads that
//       doc to tell the founder whether the deployed rules are current, and
//       it goes stale/wrong the moment this step is skipped.
//  That's it. Changes are live in ~10 seconds.
//
//  FULL REPLACE ONLY - this is a firm rule, not a suggestion: always select
//  ALL existing text in the Rules tab, delete it, then paste this entire
//  file. NEVER paste this as an addition alongside whatever text is already
//  there. Firestore evaluates every `match` block that applies to a path and
//  allows the request if ANY of them says yes (rules are unioned with OR,
//  not overridden) - so a leftover old block sitting next to a new hardened
//  one does not get replaced by it, it gets OR'd with it. That is exactly
//  how several already-fixed holes on tripchats, meta/signupCounter, claims
//  and pulse ended up live again: someone pasted a newer, stricter block in
//  without deleting the older, looser one first, and the old one silently
//  kept granting the access the new one was written to shut off. Select all,
//  delete, paste, publish - every single time.
//
//  IF THE T-SHIRT "ORDER" BUTTON ERRORS  -> the 'merch' rule below wasn't live.
//  IF STAFF LOGIN WON'T PROCEED          -> the 'staff' rule below wasn't live,
//                                           OR that account has no staff/{uid}
//                                           doc yet (add it in Firestore).
//
//  v15.10: CodeRabbit PR #60 follow-up round, 4 more findings. (1) partnerClaims/
//  {id} create now additionally requires isAdmin() OR a verified caller whose
//  own auth token email matches the claim's stated email - previously ANY
//  caller could invent an arbitrary claim (any code/email) and self-redeem it
//  for a free Pro grant with zero real partner distribution involved. NOTE:
//  nmims/index.html's claim form does not currently sign visitors in at all,
//  so this is a KNOWN BREAKING CHANGE for that page until it is updated to
//  authenticate first - see the match block below. (2) the expiresAt check
//  moved from isRedeemedByCaller() (grant time) to the partnerClaims/{id}
//  update rule's initial redemption transition (redemption time) - checking
//  it at grant time could permanently strand a legitimately on-time
//  redemption if the follow-up users/{uid} grant write failed and was retried
//  after expiry. (3) pricing/founder's update-rule comment corrected - it
//  falsely claimed the count+1 increment was batched atomically with
//  openPartnerRedeem()'s writes; that function performs two sequential
//  awaited writes, never a batch, and never touches pricing/founder at all
//  (rule logic unchanged). (4) app.js's partner-redeem profile-creation
//  set() now uses {merge:true} so it can't clobber fields written by a
//  concurrent process between the existence check and this write.
//
//  v15.9: CodeRabbit PR #60 follow-up round - three quick fixes. (1) reports/
//  {reportId} create now requires the client-supplied `created` (squad) /
//  `at` (beacon) field to actually `is timestamp` (both are always
//  FieldValue.serverTimestamp() from app.js's reportSquad()/rwBeaconReport()),
//  not just present, closing off a spoofed-value path. (2) deckleads/{id}
//  create AND update now cap `openedAt`/`updatedAt` (ISO strings from
//  deck/index.html's new Date().toISOString(), <=24 chars) at size()<=40 -
//  previously only `create` skipped validating openedAt entirely, and update
//  checked type but not length, letting an anonymous caller stuff an
//  oversized string into either field. (3) ledger/{id}'s isFinance() update
//  branch now also requires resource.data.enteredBy == request.auth.uid (in
//  addition to the existing request.resource.data check) - previously a
//  finance-role user could update ANY ledger doc, including admin-created
//  ones, as long as the new data had a permitted kind/amount and their own
//  uid on enteredBy; now a finance user can only ever touch a record they
//  themselves originally entered. isAdmin() is unaffected by all three.
//  Two other CodeRabbit findings from the same round - the partnerClaims/
//  partnerships allocation-counter TOCTOU, and pricing/founder.count having
//  no binding to a real redemption event - are pre-existing, heavy-lift,
//  architectural gaps left untouched here; see the match blocks below for
//  the standing explanation of why a rules-only patch can't fully close
//  them.
//
//  v15.7: re-audited a fresh copy of the LIVE Firebase Console rules (pasted
//  2026-08-28) against this file. Confirmed the live console still had NINE
//  duplicated `match` paths left over from past incremental pastes -
//  tripchats (x3), pulse (x3), staff (x2), squads (x2), reports (x2),
//  pricing (x2), meta/{doc} (x2), ledger (x2), claims (x2) - each unioned
//  with a newer hardened block already in this file. The two worst live,
//  currently-exploitable holes this caused: (1) a blanket
//  `tripchats/{room}: allow read, write: if request.auth != null` let ANY
//  signed-in user read/write ANY trip chat room and message, bypassing the
//  membership check entirely; (2) a leftover
//  `meta/{doc}: allow write: if doc=='signupCounter' && request.auth != null`
//  with no anti-tampering check let ANY signed-in user set or delete the
//  founder-offer signup counter to any value, reopening/closing that offer
//  at will. Both (and the other seven duplicates) are removed below - see
//  firestore-rules-history/ for the exact live snapshot this was audited
//  against and the PR that fixed it.
//
//  v15.6: reconciled this file against what was actually LIVE in the Firebase
//  Console, which had drifted ahead of this repo file with real, in-use
//  functionality (passports, beacons, partners + partner room listings,
//  roomBookings, meta/publicStats, staff logs/sessions subcollections, an
//  isStaffAdmin()/isPartner() helper pair) as well as several duplicate/
//  conflicting `match` blocks for the same collection. See the merge report
//  for the exact conflict-by-conflict resolution. Every collection either
//  side actually used is covered exactly once below.
//
//  Covers: merch (t-shirt orders), staff (team roles), users, payments,
//  ratings, reports, tripchats, passports, beacons, partners, roomBookings,
//  investors, and more. Everything the app writes to has a matching rule
//  here - deploying this fixes permission-denied.
// ============================================================================

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // You are admin only if a doc exists at admins/<your-uid>.
    // That doc can ONLY be created from the Firebase Console (no client can write it).
    function isAdmin() {
      return request.auth != null
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/admins/$(request.auth.uid));
    }
    function isSelf(uid) {
      return request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
    }
    // A partnerClaims/{code} doc grants Pro to the CALLER only after it has
    // ALREADY been flipped to redeemed by that same caller — proRedeemed
    // must be true (not merely absent/false) AND redeemedUid must equal the
    // caller's own uid. Shared by the users/{uid} partner-redeem rule and
    // documented again next to partnerClaims below.
    //
    // EXPIRY (v15.10): deliberately NOT checked here. expiresAt (a Firestore
    // Timestamp set at claim time, e.g. the NMIMS 30-day claim window in
    // nmims/index.html) is enforced ONLY at the partnerClaims/{id} update
    // rule's initial false/absent -> true redemption transition, below.
    // Checking it again here — at grant time, potentially long after the
    // redemption flip already committed — used to mean a valid, on-time
    // redemption could be permanently locked out of ever granting Pro: if
    // the users/{uid} write failed for an unrelated reason (e.g. a dropped
    // connection) and the user retried after expiresAt had since passed,
    // this function would start rejecting the retry forever, even though the
    // redemption itself legitimately happened before expiry. Once
    // redeemedUid is already bound to the caller and proRedeemed is already
    // true, that redemption already happened validly and the grant must be
    // retryable indefinitely.
    //
    // SECURITY (closes a multi-account replay hole): this used to be
    // isUnredeemedClaim(code) — true whenever proRedeemed was NOT yet true —
    // which only checked the CODE's state, never WHO was redeeming it. Since
    // Firestore rules can't force two separate document writes to happen
    // together, a client could skip the partnerClaims flip entirely and
    // write straight to users/{uid}: the code would stay "unredeemed"
    // forever, letting a second, third, Nth account replay the exact same
    // leaked code for unlimited Pro grants. Inverting the check to require
    // the claim be redeemed FIRST, as its own already-committed write, and
    // bound to this exact caller's uid, means the users/{uid} grant can only
    // ever succeed once per code, for the one account that redeemed it.
    // This still does not guarantee the two writes happen in a single
    // transaction — it guarantees that whichever account's uid got written
    // to redeemedUid is the only account that can ever pass this check for
    // that code.
    function isRedeemedByCaller(code) {
      return code is string && code.size() > 0 && code.size() <= 64
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerClaims/$(code))
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerClaims/$(code)).data.proRedeemed == true
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerClaims/$(code)).data.redeemedUid == request.auth.uid;
    }

    // ---------- USERS ----------
    // A user may create/read their own profile but can NEVER touch the pro fields.
    // Pro is set only by you (admin) - users cannot grant themselves Pro.
    match /users/{uid} {
      allow read: if isSelf(uid) || isAdmin();
      allow create: if isSelf(uid)
        && !request.resource.data.keys().hasAny(['pro','proAt','proMethod','proPayId']);
      allow update: if isAdmin();
      allow update: if isSelf(uid)
        && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasAny(['pro','proAt','proMethod','proPayId']);
      // ---- PARTNER CODE REDEMPTION (rw-v116 hardening) ----
      // The ONLY way a user may touch pro/proAt/proMethod/proCode themselves:
      // redeeming a partner code that THEY THEMSELVES have already redeemed
      // (isRedeemedByCaller, defined above via get() — rules can't run a
      // query, only look up a known doc path, which is exactly why
      // partnerClaims' doc ID is the code itself).
      //
      // ORDER MATTERS: the client must flip partnerClaims/{code}.proRedeemed
      // to true (and set redeemedUid to its own uid) as a FULLY SEPARATE,
      // AWAITED write FIRST — see openPartnerRedeem() in app.js. Only once
      // that write has actually committed does this rule's get() see it, and
      // only then can this users/{uid} grant write succeed. A single atomic
      // batch does NOT work here: get() inside a rule only ever sees already-
      // committed state, never a sibling pending write in the same batch, so
      // batching these two writes together would make this check fail every
      // time. This closes the multi-account replay gap (previously, any
      // client could grant itself Pro just by citing a not-yet-redeemed code,
      // skipping the partnerClaims flip, so the same leaked code could be
      // replayed by unlimited accounts) — it does NOT add transactional
      // atomicity between the two writes; see the comment on
      // isRedeemedByCaller() above for what guarantee this actually provides.
      allow update: if isSelf(uid)
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasOnly(['pro','proAt','proMethod','proCode'])
        && request.resource.data.pro == true
        && request.resource.data.proMethod == 'partner'
        && isRedeemedByCaller(request.resource.data.proCode);
      allow delete: if isAdmin() || isSelf(uid);

      // Device registry for the 3-device cap (account-bound Pro system).
      match /devices/{deviceId} {
        allow read, write: if isSelf(uid);
      }
    }

    // ---------- UPI CLAIMS (zero-fee flow) ----------
    // User submits their UTR after paying your QR; only admin can approve.
    // MERGE NOTE: live Firestore had TWO claims/{..} blocks - a basic one
    // (utr length 8-40, no amount check) and a later "v71" hardened one
    // (utr length ==12 exactly, amount 0<x<=100000 required, refCode format
    // checked, seatCounted forbidden on create). The hardened version is used
    // here exclusively; the basic one is dropped. Confirmed against the real
    // client (app.js ~line 10050): it validates /^\d{12}$/ before submit and
    // always sends a numeric `amount`, so the hardened shape is what the app
    // actually sends - the looser rule was strictly weaker, never needed.
    match /claims/{id} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
        && request.resource.data.status == 'pending'
        && !('seatCounted' in request.resource.data)
        && request.resource.data.amount is number
        && request.resource.data.amount > 0
        && request.resource.data.amount <= 100000
        && request.resource.data.utr is string
        && request.resource.data.utr.size() == 12
        && request.resource.data.utr.matches('^[0-9]{12}$')
        && id == request.auth.uid + '_' + request.resource.data.utr
        && (!('refCode' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.refCode is string
                && request.resource.data.refCode.size() <= 32));
      allow read: if isAdmin() || (request.auth != null && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid);
      allow update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- SYNC CIRCLE / TRAVEL PULSE (anonymous aggregate demand) ----------
    // MERGE NOTE: live had two conflicting pulse/{..} blocks - one requiring
    // `count is int` on every write, one just requiring auth. Neither alone is
    // fully correct: app.js's syncGo() writes to pulse/intents with dynamic
    // per-key increment fields and NO `count` field at all (that strict rule
    // would reject it), while pulseBump() writes to a per-key pulse doc WITH a
    // fixed {n, m, count, at} shape that should stay validated.
    //
    // Two real write shapes hit this collection, split below by doc id:
    //  - pulse/intents (id == 'intents'): syncGo() merges exactly ONE dynamic
    //    per-trip key -> FieldValue.increment(1) per call, e.g. {"paris_soon": 3}.
    //    The key name comes from free-text trip name + month, so it can't be
    //    enumerated in advance. Firestore Rules has no loop/quantifier to
    //    validate an unknown map key's value (MapDiff/Set only expose
    //    affectedKeys()/hasOnly()/hasAny() etc. over key *names*, never a way
    //    to index into the map by a name pulled from a Set) - so the actual
    //    numeric type/sign of that one dynamic field can't be checked here.
    //    What IS enforced: at most one key may change per write (matching the
    //    real client, which only ever bumps one key at a time), no existing
    //    key may be deleted, and the map can't be grown past a sane size -
    //    this bounds the blast radius even though the value itself is opaque.
    //    Closing that residual gap for real needs either a Cloud Function to
    //    validate after write, or restructuring intents as per-key
    //    subdocuments (pulse/intents/{key}) so the key becomes a path segment
    //    rules CAN see - a data-model change, intentionally not made here.
    //  - pulse/{pulseKey(name,month)} (any other id): pulseBump() writes the
    //    fixed {n, m, count, at} shape - fully validated since the field
    //    names are known.
    match /pulse/{id} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create, update: if request.auth != null
        && (
          (id == 'intents'
            && (resource == null || request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).removedKeys().size() == 0)
            && (resource == null
                  ? request.resource.data.size() == 1
                  : request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().size() == 1)
            && request.resource.data.size() <= 5000)
          || (id != 'intents'
              && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['n','m','count','at'])
              && (!('n' in request.resource.data)
                  || (request.resource.data.n is string && request.resource.data.n.size() <= 60))
              && (!('m' in request.resource.data)
                  || (request.resource.data.m is string && request.resource.data.m.size() <= 40))
              && (!('count' in request.resource.data)
                  || (request.resource.data.count is int && request.resource.data.count >= 0)))
        );
    }

    match /requests/{id} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
        && request.resource.data.text is string
        && request.resource.data.text.size() <= 200;
      allow read: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- PAYMENTS LOG ----------
    match /payments/{id} {
      allow read: if isAdmin();
      allow write: if false;
    }

    // ---------- ADMIN AUDIT LOG ----------
    // Append-only audit trail for admin-initiated Pro grants/changes made via
    // a private, separate admin console (not part of this app's client code).
    // That tool creates entries (isAdmin()), other admins may review the log,
    // but nobody - including admins - can ever edit or delete a past entry,
    // so manual Pro grants always leave a traceable record.
    match /adminAuditLog/{id} {
      allow create: if isAdmin();
      allow read: if isAdmin();
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // ---------- ADMINS ----------
    // Never writable from any client. Add your own UID manually in the Firebase Console.
    match /admins/{uid} {
      allow read: if isSelf(uid);
      // You may set ONLY your own pinHash (the second-factor lock). You can
      // never grant yourself admin status - that stays Console-only.
      allow update: if isSelf(uid)
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['pinHash']);
    }

    // ---------- STATS (anonymous daily funnel counters) ----------
    // WAS: `allow write: if true` - literally any caller, signed in or not,
    // could set/overwrite ANY field to ANY value on ANY day's doc, or delete
    // it outright. Verified against the actual client (app.js's track(),
    // ~line 5643): it always does
    // `db.collection('stats').doc(day).set({ev: FieldValue.increment(1)},
    // {merge:true})` for exactly one of a fixed set of known event names -
    // and it deliberately does NOT check `user`/sign-in state, since events
    // like 'visits' and 'searches' must be counted for anonymous, pre-login
    // traffic too. So this can't be locked to request.auth != null without
    // breaking real anonymous funnel tracking - instead the SHAPE is locked
    // down: exactly one of these known keys, moved by exactly +1 from
    // whatever it already was (0 if new), same anti-tampering pattern as
    // meta/signupCounter below. No caller, signed in or not, can inject an
    // unknown field or jam an arbitrary value into any day's doc anymore.
    function statsBump(key) {
      return request.resource.data.diff(resource == null ? {} : resource.data)
               .affectedKeys().hasOnly([key])
        && request.resource.data[key] is int
        && request.resource.data[key] ==
             ((resource != null && key in resource.data && resource.data[key] is int)
               ? resource.data[key] + 1 : 1);
    }
    match /stats/{day} {
      allow read: if isAdmin();
      allow create, update: if statsBump('arena_runs') || statsBump('atlas_cert_made')
        || statsBump('aff_click') || statsBump('copilot_uses') || statsBump('event_plans')
        || statsBump('img_scans') || statsBump('nudge_shown') || statsBump('nudge_yes')
        || statsBump('pay_opens') || statsBump('pdf_generated') || statsBump('pdf_opens')
        || statsBump('pdf_paid') || statsBump('pwa_dismissed') || statsBump('pwa_installed')
        || statsBump('ref_click') || statsBump('ref_code_entered') || statsBump('searches')
        || statsBump('signups') || statsBump('trip_saved') || statsBump('tusk_helpful')
        || statsBump('tusk_unhelpful') || statsBump('utr_submits') || statsBump('video_made')
        || statsBump('video_opens') || statsBump('visits') || statsBump('wiz_opens');
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- RATINGS & TESTIMONIALS ----------
    // Public read (needed for the average-score + testimonial wall). One doc per
    // user (doc id = uid), so "one rating per account" is enforced server-side.
    match /ratings/{uid} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create, update: if isSelf(uid)
        && request.resource.data.stars is int
        && request.resource.data.stars >= 1
        && request.resource.data.stars <= 5
        && (!('text' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.text.size() <= 200);
      allow delete: if isSelf(uid);
    }

    // ---------- TRIP SQUADS ----------
    // Safe, destination-anchored posts. Public read, owner-only delete, immutable
    // once created (delete + repost instead of editing in place).
    // MERGE NOTE: live had a second, near-duplicate squads/{post} block that
    // required auth just to READ (auth!=null instead of true). Not a security
    // hole either way, but the app's own openSquads() already requires
    // sign-in client-side before it ever queries squads (app.js), so nothing
    // actually depends on the read being auth-gated at the rules layer. Kept
    // the more open, previously-documented `read: if true` and dropped the
    // stricter duplicate.
    match /squads/{squadId} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
        && request.resource.data.note.size() <= 140
        && (!('contact' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.contact.size() <= 80);
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid;
      allow update: if false;
    }

    // ---------- REPORTS (moderation flags) ----------
    // Write-only from clients (including anonymous - safety reports shouldn't
    // require an account); only admin can read/manage them.
    // MERGE NOTE: live had a conflicting reports/{r} duplicate requiring auth
    // to create AND blocking read/update/delete outright, even for admin
    // (`if false`) - that would make submitted reports permanently
    // unreadable by anyone, contradicting the documented "anonymous safety
    // report" intent of the first block. Dropped the duplicate; kept this one.
    //
    // WAS: `allow create: if true` with zero shape check - any caller could
    // write an arbitrarily-shaped, arbitrarily-large doc here. Verified
    // against the two real client write paths (app.js): reportSquad() sends
    // {type:'squad', targetId, by, created}; rwBeaconReport() sends
    // {kind:'beacon', target, why, by, at}. Neither matches the sibling
    // abuse/{id} collection's 'reason' field name, so this validates the
    // actual shapes instead - free text and identifiers are size-capped and
    // no extra field can be smuggled in.
    match /reports/{reportId} {
      allow create: if
           (request.resource.data.type == 'squad'
             && request.resource.data.targetId is string
             && request.resource.data.targetId.size() <= 200
             && request.resource.data.by is string
             && request.resource.data.by.size() <= 128
             && request.resource.data.created is timestamp
             && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['type','targetId','by','created']))
        || (request.resource.data.kind == 'beacon'
             && request.resource.data.target is string
             && request.resource.data.target.size() <= 200
             && (!('why' in request.resource.data)
                 || (request.resource.data.why is string && request.resource.data.why.size() <= 600))
             && (request.resource.data.by == null
                 || (request.resource.data.by is string && request.resource.data.by.size() <= 128))
             && request.resource.data.at is timestamp
             && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['kind','target','why','by','at']));
      allow read, update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- TRIP CHATS (private group rooms) ----------
    // Readable/writable ONLY by uids listed on the room's members array - this
    // is the actual security boundary, enforced here not just in the UI. The
    // room is created by a signed-in user who must include themselves; joining
    // appends your own uid. Messages are per-member, signed with the sender uid.
    //
    // *** SECURITY: live Firestore ALSO had a second, blanket tripchats/{room}
    // block: `allow read, write: if request.auth != null` for the room doc AND
    // its msgs subcollection - i.e. ANY signed-in user (not just members) could
    // read or write ANY trip chat room and its messages, completely bypassing
    // the membership check this block enforces. That duplicate is a real,
    // currently-live hole and has been REMOVED ENTIRELY. Only this
    // member-checked version is kept. ***
    match /tripchats/{roomId} {
      // NOTE: resource is NULL for a document that doesn't exist yet, so the old
      // rule made the existence check itself fail - tripChatOpen() does a
      // .get() first to decide whether to create the room, and that .get()
      // was permission-denied every time. Result: group chat never opened.
      allow read: if request.auth != null
        && (resource == null || request.auth.uid in resource.data.members);
      allow create: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid in request.resource.data.members;
      // only let a signed-in, non-banned user add THEMSELVES to members (join
      // via a shared room-id/link, see chatInvite() / tripChatOpen() in
      // app.js), nothing else destructive.
      //
      // SECURITY: previously this only checked (a) the requester ends up IN
      // the new members list and (b) no existing member was removed. It never
      // checked that the requester was already a member OR that the only uid
      // being added was their own, and never checked isBanned() - so ANY
      // signed-in user (including a banned one) could add themselves, or
      // anyone else, to ANY existing room's members array and gain full
      // read/write access to that private chat. Fixed by additionally
      // requiring !isBanned() and that the set of uids being added is empty
      // or exactly {request.auth.uid} (i.e. you can only ever add yourself).
      //
      // SECURITY: the members-array checks above never restricted WHICH
      // fields the update could touch, so a member could ride along a valid
      // self-join with a change to any other room field (name/owner/etc) in
      // the same write. Fixed by requiring the diff only ever touches
      // 'members'.
      allow update: if request.auth != null
        && !isBanned()
        && request.auth.uid in request.resource.data.members
        && resource.data.members.toSet().difference(request.resource.data.members.toSet()).size() == 0
        && request.resource.data.members.toSet().difference(resource.data.members.toSet())
             .difference([request.auth.uid].toSet()).size() == 0
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['members']);
      match /msgs/{msgId} {
        // LATENT BUG FIXED: get() on a room that does not exist returns null,
        // and reading .data.members off null makes the whole rule evaluation
        // fail - which surfaces to the user as "Missing or insufficient
        // permissions" even when they are a legitimate member. Guard with
        // exists() FIRST; && short-circuits, so get() only runs on a real doc.
        function roomExists() {
          return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/tripchats/$(roomId));
        }
        function isMember() {
          return request.auth.uid in
            get(/databases/$(database)/documents/tripchats/$(roomId)).data.members;
        }
        allow read: if request.auth != null && roomExists() && isMember();
        allow create: if request.auth != null
          && !isBanned()
          && roomExists()
          && isMember()
          && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
          && request.resource.data.text is string
          && request.resource.data.text.size() <= 1000
          // structured messages (budget/itinerary/poll/meet/paid/tusk) carry a
          // small payload; cap the kind so nothing arbitrary is stored
          && (!('kind' in request.resource.data)
              || request.resource.data.kind in ['text','tusk','budget','plan','meet','poll','paid']);
        allow delete: if false;
        allow update: if request.auth != null
          && !isBanned()
          && roomExists()
          && isMember()
          && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['reactions']);
      }
    }

    // ---------- RULES VERSION PROBE ----------
    // Public-readable, admin-written. Lets the app tell the founder definitively
    // whether the deployed rules are current, instead of everyone guessing when
    // a feature returns "insufficient permissions".
    match /meta/rulesVersion {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- SECRETS (end-to-end encrypted API-key backup) ----------
    // Ciphertext only: encrypted in the user's browser with a passphrase we
    // never receive. Strictly owner-only - no admin read, because there is
    // no legitimate reason for anyone but the user to touch this document.
    match /secrets/{uid} {
      allow read, write, delete: if isSelf(uid);
    }

    // ---------- CONFIG (owner-managed app settings) ----------
    // Affiliate IDs, Gumroad link/ID, WhatsApp numbers, wallets, promo URL.
    // Publicly readable (the app needs them), writable ONLY by admin - this
    // must NOT live in the meta collection, which any signed-in user can write.
    match /config/{doc} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- CRM (contact database: personal data, admin-only) ----------
    // Never publicly readable - it holds names and emails of real people.
    match /crm/{id} {
      allow read, update, delete: if isAdmin();
      // Marketing intern: creator-partnership records only, and only to move
      // outreach along. They never see other CRM segments (investors, etc).
      allow read: if isMarketing() && resource.data.seg == 'creator';
      allow update: if isMarketing()
        && resource.data.seg == 'creator'
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasOnly(['stage','note','lastPitch','lastPitchAt']);
      // Inbound creator applications from /creators/ - anyone may CREATE, but
      // only with consent explicitly true and marked inbound, and nobody but an
      // admin can ever read the collection back. This is deliberately the only
      // way personal contact data enters the CRM without the founder typing it:
      // the person opts in themselves. Size caps stop the form being abused as
      // free storage.
      allow create: if isAdmin() || (
           request.resource.data.seg == 'creator'
        && request.resource.data.consent == true
        && request.resource.data.source == 'creators page (inbound)'
        && request.resource.data.stage == 'new'
        && request.resource.data.name is string  && request.resource.data.name.size()  <= 120
        && request.resource.data.email is string && request.resource.data.email.size() <= 160
        && request.resource.data.note is string  && request.resource.data.note.size()  <= 1200
        // An applicant must NOT be able to award themselves the ladder. Tier,
        // referral counts and the affiliate code are set by the founder only -
        // otherwise anyone could POST tier:'featured' and claim Pro for life.
        && request.resource.data.tier == 'applied'
        && request.resource.data.refSales == 0
        && request.resource.data.refRevenue == 0
        && (!('code' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.code == '')
        && (!('proGranted' in request.resource.data))
      );
    }

    // ---------- STAFF ROLES (least-privilege for interns) ----------
    // The founder is the only true admin (admins/{uid}). Interns get a role
    // that unlocks exactly what their job needs and nothing else. Critically:
    // NEITHER intern role can read users, secrets, tripchats or raw payment
    // records - those hold personal data and API keys that no intern needs
    // to do marketing or bookkeeping. Access is proven by a staff doc the
    // founder (or another staff-admin) creates/activates.
    function staffRole() {
      return request.auth != null
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/staff/$(request.auth.uid))
        ? get(/databases/$(database)/documents/staff/$(request.auth.uid)).data.role
        : '';
    }
    function isMarketing() { return staffRole() == 'marketing'; }
    function isFinance()   { return staffRole() == 'finance'; }
    function isStaff()     { return isMarketing() || isFinance(); }
    // A SEPARATE, narrower admin concept from isAdmin(): someone whose staff/
    // record itself carries role=='admin' (e.g. an ops lead promoted in the
    // staff system) rather than a true Console-managed admins/{uid} doc. This
    // was live already (as a second, standalone staff/{uid} block) and is
    // genuinely needed - staff/index.html's "founder view" (hrLoad(),
    // internRefresh()) runs `db.collection('staff').get()`, an unfiltered
    // LIST across the whole collection, gated client-side to ROLE==='admin'
    // (derived from this exact field). A plain isSelf(uid) check cannot make
    // that list succeed for any doc but the caller's own, so a role-blind
    // "any signed-in user" rule is not what the feature needs - only
    // isAdmin() or isStaffAdmin() is.
    function isStaffAdmin() {
      return request.auth != null
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/staff/$(request.auth.uid))
        && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/staff/$(request.auth.uid)).data.role == 'admin';
    }

    // ---------- STAFF ----------
    // MERGE NOTE (changes live behavior - read carefully): live Firestore had
    // TWO staff/{uid} blocks that genuinely conflicted, not just in
    // permissiveness:
    //   1) admin-or-self read, create requires role=='none', update/delete
    //      admin-only (no self-update at all).
    //   2) ANY signed-in user (not just staff) could read the ENTIRE staff
    //      roster; create required the `role` field to be ABSENT rather than
    //      =='none'; update allowed isStaffAdmin() OR self-update of
    //      everything except role/docs/staffId; plus logs/ and sessions/
    //      subcollections with their own rules (not present in block 1, or
    //      anywhere in the repo file, at all).
    // Resolution, checked against the real client (staff/index.html):
    //   - registerPending() (the actual first-login self-registration write)
    //     sends `role:'none'` EXPLICITLY, not an absent field - so block (2)'s
    //     create condition would have REJECTED every real signup. Block (1)'s
    //     `role=='none'` check is what the client actually satisfies; kept.
    //   - The only places that `.get()` the whole staff collection
    //     (hrLoad(), internRefresh()) are gated to ROLE==='admin' in the UI -
    //     never to "any signed-in user". Block (2)'s blanket
    //     `read: if auth!=null` for literally everyone (including ordinary,
    //     non-staff app users) is broader than the feature needs and is
    //     dropped; isAdmin()/isStaffAdmin()/self covers every real caller.
    //   - Self-service edits ARE real and currently used
    //     (deskAckHandbook -> handbookAckAt, deskToggleResource ->
    //     resourceProgress.*, planDone), all via set({...},{merge:true}) on
    //     the caller's OWN doc. Block (1)'s admin-only update would break all
    //     of these today. Block (2)'s self-update-except-role/docs/staffId
    //     carve-out is the one actually needed; adopted here alongside
    //     isAdmin()/isStaffAdmin() full-update access (role activation,
    //     docs/sheet edits by the founder/staff-admin - see
    //     internSaveAdd/internSaveSheet/internSaveDocs, all called only from
    //     the admin-gated founder-view UI).
    //   - logs/ and sessions/ subcollections are real, actively read/written
    //     by the client (deskPaint's own-log/session reads, internRefresh's
    //     per-person log/session rollups) and had NO rule anywhere in the
    //     repo file - carried over from live as-is, with isAdmin() unioned
    //     into the staff-admin checks for consistency with the rest of this
    //     file (every other collection lets the true founder admin in too).
    match /staff/{uid} {
      allow read: if isAdmin() || isStaffAdmin() || isSelf(uid);
      allow create: if isAdmin() ||
        (isSelf(uid) && request.resource.data.role == 'none');
      allow update: if isAdmin() || isStaffAdmin() || (
        isSelf(uid)
        && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasAny(['role','docs','staffId'])
      );
      allow delete: if isAdmin() || isStaffAdmin();

      match /logs/{day} {
        allow read: if isSelf(uid) || isAdmin() || isStaffAdmin();
        allow create, update: if isSelf(uid);
        allow delete: if isAdmin();
      }
      match /sessions/{day} {
        allow read: if isSelf(uid) || isAdmin() || isStaffAdmin();
        allow create, update: if isSelf(uid);
        allow delete: if isAdmin() || isStaffAdmin();
      }
    }

    // ---------- TASKS (the intern work board) ----------
    match /tasks/{id} {
      allow read: if isAdmin() || isStaff();
      allow create, delete: if isAdmin();
      // Staff may only move their OWN task along and add notes - they cannot
      // reassign work, change the brief, or edit someone else's task.
      allow update: if isAdmin() || (
           isStaff()
        && resource.data.assignee == staffRole()
        && request.resource.data.assignee == resource.data.assignee
        && request.resource.data.title == resource.data.title
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasOnly(['status','note','updatedAt','proofUrl'])
      );
    }

    // ---------- LEDGER (finance intern: aggregates only) ----------
    // Deliberately a SEPARATE collection of summary rows the founder writes,
    // rather than opening the raw claims/payments collections - those carry
    // payer identities and UPI references an intern has no business seeing.
    // MERGE NOTE: live had a second, more restrictive ledger/{entryId} block
    // (admin-only create, update/delete always false) that would silently
    // remove the finance intern's ability to log expenses at all. This repo
    // file's existing version already matches live's FIRST, feature-complete
    // ledger/{id} block, so it's kept unchanged and the restrictive duplicate
    // is dropped.
    match /ledger/{id} {
      allow read: if isAdmin() || isFinance();
      allow create, update: if isAdmin() || (
           isFinance()
        && request.resource.data.kind in ['expense','note']
        && request.resource.data.amount is number
        && request.resource.data.amount <= 100000
        && request.resource.data.enteredBy == request.auth.uid
        // A finance-role (non-admin) caller may only ever touch a record
        // they themselves entered - on create resource is null (nothing to
        // own yet), but on update the EXISTING doc's enteredBy must already
        // match this caller, otherwise a finance user could overwrite
        // someone else's (including an admin's) ledger entry just by
        // supplying a permitted kind/amount and their own uid.
        && (resource == null || resource.data.enteredBy == request.auth.uid)
      );
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- BANS (enforced server-side, not just in the UI) ----------
    // A ban has to bite at the DATA layer. If it only hides buttons, anyone who
    // opens devtools is back in. isBanned() is called by every social write
    // below, so a banned uid cannot post, chat, join a squad or report.
    function isBanned() {
      return exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/bans/$(request.auth.uid));
    }
    match /bans/{uid} {
      allow read: if isAdmin() || (request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid);
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- REPORTS (abuse reports from travellers) ----------
    match /abuse/{id} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null
        && !isBanned()
        && request.resource.data.reporter == request.auth.uid
        && request.resource.data.reason is string
        && request.resource.data.reason.size() <= 600
        && request.resource.data.status == 'open';
      allow read, update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- MERCH ORDERS ----------
    match /merch/{id} {
      allow create: if request.auth != null && !isBanned()
        && request.resource.data.status == 'new'
        && request.resource.data.slogan is string && request.resource.data.slogan.size() <= 120
        && request.resource.data.addr is string   && request.resource.data.addr.size() <= 400;
      allow read: if isAdmin() || (request.auth != null && resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid);
      allow update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---------- META (shared counters + the founder-offer gate) ----------
    // WAS: allow write when request.auth != null - which let ANY signed-in
    // user rewrite the founder signup counter: set it to 0 to keep the Rs 100
    // lifetime offer open forever, or to a huge number to slam it shut. The
    // counter is now increment-by-exactly-one and can never be lowered.
    //
    // MERGE NOTE (security regression risk, avoided): live had a second,
    // looser meta/{doc} block whose signupCounter write rule dropped the
    // increment-by-exactly-1 anti-tampering check entirely (`write: if
    // doc=='signupCounter' && auth!=null`, no comparison against the prior
    // value) - i.e. it reopened exactly the exploit this block's own comment
    // says was deliberately closed. That duplicate is dropped; this stricter
    // block is kept as-is. Its `founderSeats` carve-out (`write: if
    // doc=='founderSeats' && isAdmin()`) needed nothing folding in - any
    // admin can already create/update/delete ANY meta doc including
    // founderSeats via the isAdmin() branches below, so it was already fully
    // covered and redundant, not a missing capability.
    match /meta/{doc} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create: if isAdmin();
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
      allow update: if isAdmin() || (
        // the ONLY self-serve write: bump the signup counter by exactly 1
        doc == 'signupCounter'
        && request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.count is int
        && request.resource.data.count == resource.data.count + 1
        && request.resource.data.keys().hasOnly(['count'])
      );
    }

    // ---------- PRICING GATE (server truth, not the device clock) ----------
    // The founder window used to be judged client-side from daysSinceLaunch(),
    // which reads the PHONE's clock - set the date back and the offer reopens.
    // Admin-owned document; everyone reads it, only an admin can move it.
    // MERGE NOTE: live had a plain-admin-only duplicate of this same doc path
    // with no founder-count carve-out; this repo version is a strict superset
    // (adds the narrow +1 counter carve-out below) so the live duplicate is
    // simply dropped as redundant.
    match /pricing/{doc} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
      // FIXED: partner-redeemed seats (e.g. NMIMS's 500 free grants) never
      // counted toward the combined 1000-account founder-era cap, because
      // this doc was admin-only and openPartnerRedeem() had nowhere safe to
      // write. Narrow carve-out, same shape as meta/signupCounter and the
      // partnerships/{id} counters elsewhere in this file: a signed-in user
      // may move ONLY 'founder'.count, ONLY by exactly +1, nothing else on
      // this doc, ever.
      //
      // CORRECTED (v15.10): the comment here previously claimed this
      // increment was "batched atomically with the matching users/{uid} and
      // partnerClaims/{code} writes in openPartnerRedeem()". That was never
      // true: openPartnerRedeem() (app.js) performs two separate SEQUENTIAL
      // awaited writes - first partnerClaims/{code}.update(), then, only
      // after that resolves, users/{uid}.update() - never a single atomic
      // batch, and it never touches pricing/founder at all. As of this
      // version no client code moves 'founder'.count; this carve-out exists
      // for a future write path, not a currently-exercised one.
      allow update: if doc == 'founder'
        && request.auth != null
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['count'])
        && request.resource.data.count == (resource.data.count is int ? resource.data.count : 0) + 1;
    }

    // ---- CROWD REPORTS (rw-v113) — Travel & Earn: Crowd Spotter ----
    // Any user (signed-in or anonymous) may create. Nobody can read each other's.
    // Admin reads for crowd calendar aggregation. No personal data in the doc.
    match /crowdReports/{id} {
      allow create: if request.resource.data.level is int
                    && request.resource.data.level >= 1
                    && request.resource.data.level <= 5
                    && request.resource.data.place is string;
      allow read: if isAdmin();
      allow update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- PARTNER CLAIMS (institution barter partnerships) ----
    // Doc ID IS the claim code itself (e.g. partnerClaims/NMIMS-A1B2C3) - not
    // an auto ID or an email-derived key. That is deliberate: rules can never
    // run an arbitrary query, only look up a single doc by a KNOWN path, so
    // making the code the path is what lets this file (and users/{uid} above,
    // via isRedeemedByCaller()) validate "is this specific code real and
    // already redeemed by this caller" with get(). `get` (fetch by known id)
    // is public because the code itself IS the shared secret / capability
    // token needed to redeem it
    // - nobody can discover a code by listing, only by already having been
    // given one (emailed after claiming). `list` stays admin-only so the
    // collection can never be enumerated or scraped.
    //
    // Personal data (name, email, phone) shared only with explicit consent.
    //
    // SECURITY (v15.10, closes a self-service Pro grant hole): create used to
    // have no auth requirement at all beyond field-shape validation, so ANY
    // caller (signed in or not) could invent a claim doc for an arbitrary
    // code/email of their choosing, immediately flip it via the update rule
    // below to proRedeemed:true/redeemedUid:self, then grant themselves Pro
    // via users/{uid}'s isRedeemedByCaller() path - entirely bypassing the
    // "a real partner claimant received this code by email" model. create now
    // additionally requires isAdmin() OR a VERIFIED Firebase Auth caller whose
    // own token email matches the claim's stated email, so a caller can only
    // ever create a claim under their own verified identity, never someone
    // else's.
    //
    // KNOWN BREAKING CHANGE - NOT YET RESOLVED: nmims/index.html's claim form
    // (submitClaim(), the only real client that creates these docs) never
    // signs the visitor in at all (no firebase-auth-compat.js is even loaded
    // on that page), so as of this rule every real submission from that page
    // will now be rejected with permission-denied until that page is updated
    // to sign the visitor in (e.g. Google sign-in, which sets
    // token.email_verified=true automatically) before calling
    // partnerClaims.doc(code).set(...). That client-side change is
    // intentionally NOT made in this pass - see the fix notes for this
    // version.
    //
    // KNOWN LIMITATION (documented, not silently ignored): the cap check
    // below reads partnerships/{partnership}.claimed via get(), but nothing
    // in these rules can FORCE a create() here to be paired with actually
    // incrementing that counter - Firestore rules cannot enforce "these two
    // writes must happen together" without a Cloud Function/transaction, and
    // this project has neither (see PRICING-REFERRAL-MATH.md /
    // Architecture-Migration-Guide for the backend's current shape). The real
    // client (nmims/index.html) always batches the partnerClaims create, the
    // partnerClaimEmails index create, and the partnerships increment into
    // ONE atomic batch.commit(), so in normal operation the counter and the
    // claim count never drift. A determined attacker hitting the REST API
    // directly could still create claim docs without incrementing the
    // counter - bounded by whatever `claimed` happens to read at the time
    // (never literally unlimited, since the cap check still runs), but not
    // fully closed without a Cloud Function. Out of scope for this pass.
    match /partnerClaims/{id} {
      allow get: if true;
      allow list: if isAdmin();
      allow create: if (isAdmin() || (
                         request.auth != null
                      && request.auth.token.email_verified == true
                      && request.auth.token.email.lower() == request.resource.data.email.lower()
                    ))
                    && request.resource.data.name is string
                    && request.resource.data.name.size() <= 120
                    && request.resource.data.email is string
                    && request.resource.data.email.size() <= 160
                    && request.resource.data.consent == true
                    && request.resource.data.partnership is string
                    && id == request.resource.data.code
                    && (!('proRedeemed' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.proRedeemed == false)
                    && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerships/$(request.resource.data.partnership))
                    && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerships/$(request.resource.data.partnership)).data.claimed
                         < (get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerships/$(request.resource.data.partnership)).data.cap is int
                            ? get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partnerships/$(request.resource.data.partnership)).data.cap
                            : 500);
      // Redemption flip (rw-v116 hardening): a signed-in user may mark the
      // claim they are personally redeeming as used - ONLY false/absent -> true,
      // ONLY these three fields, and only recording their OWN uid, never
      // someone else's. This MUST commit before the matching users/{uid}
      // pro-grant write is even attempted (see openPartnerRedeem() in app.js
      // and the comment on isRedeemedByCaller() above) - only once this write
      // has landed does isRedeemedByCaller() see redeemedUid==this caller and
      // let the grant through, and it can never approve the same code for a
      // second account since redeemedUid is now fixed to whoever redeemed it
      // first.
      //
      // EXPIRY (v15.10): checked HERE, on the initial false/absent -> true
      // transition only, not in isRedeemedByCaller() / at grant time. This is
      // the step that actually represents "the redemption happened" - once it
      // has committed, that action is done and valid, regardless of whether
      // expiresAt later passes before the (separate, retryable) users/{uid}
      // grant write succeeds. Checking expiry again at grant time used to let
      // a legitimate, on-time redemption get permanently stuck if the grant
      // write failed for an unrelated reason and the retry landed after
      // expiresAt - see isRedeemedByCaller() above for the full history.
      allow update: if isAdmin() || (
           request.auth != null
        && (!('proRedeemed' in resource.data) || resource.data.proRedeemed == false)
        && request.resource.data.proRedeemed == true
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasOnly(['proRedeemed','redeemedAt','redeemedUid'])
        && request.resource.data.redeemedUid == request.auth.uid
        && (!('expiresAt' in resource.data) || resource.data.expiresAt > request.time)
      );
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- PARTNER CLAIM EMAIL INDEX (dedup lookup, no personal data beyond
    // the key itself) ----
    // Needed because locking partnerClaims down to get-by-code-only (above)
    // means the claim page can no longer run a `where('email','==',...)`
    // query to detect "you already claimed" (that's a list, and list is
    // admin-only now). Doc ID = a sanitized, lowercased email, so a repeat
    // submission can look itself up with a single get(). Firestore's create
    // vs. update distinction does the dedup enforcement for free: a second
    // attempt to write the SAME doc ID is an update, and only admin can
    // update this collection, so a second claim under the same email is
    // rejected rather than overwriting the first person's code.
    match /partnerClaimEmails/{emailKey} {
      allow get: if true;
      allow list: if isAdmin();
      allow create: if request.resource.data.code is string
                    && request.resource.data.code.size() <= 64
                    && request.resource.data.partnership is string;
      allow update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- PARTNERSHIPS (allocation tracking — counters only, no personal data) ----
    // Public read so the claim page can show live seat count.
    // Only admin can create the doc / set caps and initial state.
    //
    // Self-serve update is deliberately narrow (rw-v115 hardening — this used
    // to accept ANY value for these fields, so any visitor with devtools open
    // could zero the counter to reopen a "closed" pool, or inflate it to
    // block real claims before the pool was full - the actual TOCTOU race
    // this comment used to warn about without fixing). Now: exactly one of
    // studentClaimed/organiserClaimed may move, only by +1, only alongside
    // `claimed` moving by the same +1, and never past that role's own cap
    // (defaults 450/50/500 match the client's hardcoded fallbacks if an admin
    // hasn't set studentCap/organiserCap/cap explicitly). Firestore applies
    // FieldValue.increment() transforms against the latest committed value at
    // write time and serializes writes to a single document, so two
    // concurrent claims are evaluated in order against the real prior value -
    // the 2nd one to land sees the 1st one's result already applied, closing
    // the race that let concurrent claims both slip in over the cap.
    match /partnerships/{id} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create: if isAdmin();
      allow update: if isAdmin() || (
           resource.data.claimed is int
        && request.resource.data.claimed == resource.data.claimed + 1
        && request.resource.data.claimed <= (resource.data.cap is int ? resource.data.cap : 500)
        && (
             (request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['claimed','studentClaimed','lastClaimedAt'])
               && resource.data.studentClaimed is int
               && request.resource.data.studentClaimed == resource.data.studentClaimed + 1
               && request.resource.data.studentClaimed <= (resource.data.studentCap is int ? resource.data.studentCap : 450))
          || (request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['claimed','organiserClaimed','lastClaimedAt'])
               && resource.data.organiserClaimed is int
               && request.resource.data.organiserClaimed == resource.data.organiserClaimed + 1
               && request.resource.data.organiserClaimed <= (resource.data.organiserCap is int ? resource.data.organiserCap : 50))
           )
      );
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- REFERRAL SIGNUP LOG (rw-v112) ----------------------------
    // Write-only for the app (no auth required — fires on new account).
    // No personal data stored — only the ref code and the new user's UID.
    //
    // WAS: comment claimed "Staff can read their OWN code's signups only",
    // but the actual condition (isAdmin() || any signed-in user, so long as
    // resource.data.code was non-null AND config/referrers merely EXISTS)
    // let ANY signed-in app user read EVERY signup record - no per-code
    // ownership check at all. True ownership lives in config/referrers'
    // `list` array of {uid, code, ...} maps (see rwRefSync() in app.js) -
    // Firestore Rules has no predicate/loop to test "does this array contain
    // an entry matching (request.auth.uid, resource.data.code)" without
    // restructuring that mapping into a per-uid doc rules could look up by
    // path, which is beyond this pass. Minimal safe fix: read is admin-only
    // now, matching the comment to the actual (tightened) behavior. Data
    // here is low-sensitivity (only a ref code + the referred user's UID, no
    // PII), so this is a conservative call either way - but note it means
    // staff/index.html's refStatsLoad() self-serve "my referral stats" query
    // (a where('code','==',myCode) list against this collection) now gets
    // permission-denied for non-admin staff. Flagged as a follow-up:
    // restoring self-serve stats needs either a per-uid mapping doc or a
    // Cloud Function to serve the aggregate.
    match /refSignups/{id} {
      allow create: if request.resource.data.code is string
                    && request.resource.data.userUID is string;
      allow read: if isAdmin();
      allow update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- INVESTOR SUBMISSIONS ----------------------------------------
    // Write-only for visitors: no sign-in required to express interest.
    // Only the founder can read submissions (private contact details).
    // A separate /meta/investorStats document carries the public board stats.
    // (Identical in the repo file and live Firestore already - no conflict.)
    match /investors/{id} {
      allow read: if isAdmin();
      allow create: if request.resource.data.amount is number
                    && request.resource.data.amount > 0
                    && request.resource.data.status == 'interested'
                    && request.resource.data.name is string
                    && request.resource.data.email is string;
      allow update, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- INVESTOR BOARD STATS (numbers only, no names/emails) ----------
    // (Identical in the repo file and live Firestore already - no conflict.)
    match /meta/investorStats {
      allow read: if true;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- PUBLIC STATS (aggregate numbers shown on marketing pages) ----
    // Live-only addition, carried over as-is. The `request.auth != null`
    // read below is redundant: the wildcard meta/{doc} block above already
    // grants `allow read: if true` for every meta doc including this one,
    // and rules are OR'd, so this doc is actually PUBLICLY readable with no
    // sign-in required - matching live.html, which reads it unauthenticated.
    // Admin-only write, no personal data.
    match /meta/publicStats {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;
      allow write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // Anyone opening the private deck link can create/update THEIR lead doc
    // (name, role, contact, scroll depth, time). Only admins can read them.
    //
    // WAS: `allow update: if resource != null` - any caller, unauthenticated,
    // could set ANY field to ANY value/size on ANY existing lead by id, with
    // zero shape check. Verified against the real client (deck/index.html):
    // the gate form re-sends {name,contact,role,source,openedAt,slides} once
    // a visitor identifies themselves (an UPDATE, since the anonymous
    // tracking doc already exists by then), and the silent tracker separately
    // updates {scrollPct}, {lastSlide}, and {timeSeconds,updatedAt}. So
    // name/contact genuinely ARE updated after create here (unlike the
    // suggested "never touch identity fields" shape) - locked instead to
    // this real field set with the same size caps as create. There is still
    // no per-lead ownership check (the deck page has no auth), so a caller
    // who already knows/guesses a specific lead id could still overwrite
    // that lead's name/contact with junk - a lower-severity gap left open on
    // purpose here (same class of limitation already documented elsewhere in
    // this file for partnerClaims/partnerships), since closing it fully
    // needs either auth on the deck page or a Cloud Function.
    match /deckleads/{id} {
      allow create: if request.resource.data.name is string
        && request.resource.data.name.size() <= 120
        && request.resource.data.contact is string
        && request.resource.data.contact.size() <= 160
        && (!('openedAt' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.openedAt is string && request.resource.data.openedAt.size() <= 40));
      allow update: if resource != null
        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
             .hasOnly(['name','contact','role','source','openedAt','slides',
                       'scrollPct','lastSlide','timeSeconds','updatedAt'])
        && (!('name' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.name is string && request.resource.data.name.size() <= 120))
        && (!('contact' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.contact is string && request.resource.data.contact.size() <= 160))
        && (!('role' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.role is string && request.resource.data.role.size() <= 120))
        && (!('source' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.source is string && request.resource.data.source.size() <= 120))
        && (!('openedAt' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.openedAt is string && request.resource.data.openedAt.size() <= 40))
        && (!('updatedAt' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.updatedAt is string && request.resource.data.updatedAt.size() <= 40))
        && (!('slides' in request.resource.data) || request.resource.data.slides is int)
        && (!('scrollPct' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.scrollPct is int
                && request.resource.data.scrollPct >= 0 && request.resource.data.scrollPct <= 100))
        && (!('lastSlide' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.lastSlide is int && request.resource.data.lastSlide >= 0))
        && (!('timeSeconds' in request.resource.data)
            || (request.resource.data.timeSeconds is int && request.resource.data.timeSeconds >= 0));
      allow read, delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- HR DOCUMENTS (offer letters, salary slips, etc.) admin only ----
    match /hrdocs/{id} {
      allow read, write: if isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- TRAVEL PASSPORTS (visited-place stamps) ----------------------
    // Live-only addition, carried over as-is: public read (the passport is a
    // shareable, public-facing badge), owner-only create, immutable
    // afterwards (no update/delete from the client).
    match /passports/{id} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create: if request.auth != null
                    && request.resource.data.uid == request.auth.uid
                    && request.resource.data.place is string
                    && request.resource.data.place.size() > 0
                    && request.resource.data.place.size() < 120
                    && request.resource.data.verified is bool;
      allow update, delete: if false;
    }

    // ---- LIVE BEACONS (real-time "I'm here" location pins) -------------
    // Live-only addition, carried over as-is: signed-in read, strictly
    // owner-only write of one's own beacon doc (doc id == uid), with a
    // required lat/lon pair.
    match /beacons/{uid} {
      allow read: if request.auth != null;
      allow create, update: if request.auth != null
                    && request.auth.uid == uid
                    && request.resource.data.uid == uid
                    && request.resource.data.lat is number
                    && request.resource.data.lon is number;
      allow delete: if request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
    }

    // A partner (hotel/hostel/tour operator) account is proven by owning the
    // partners/{uid} doc itself - doc id == the partner's own auth uid.
    function isPartner(uid) {
      return request.auth != null && request.auth.uid == uid;
    }

    // ---- PARTNERS (hotel/hostel/operator accounts) ---------------------
    // Live-only addition, carried over as-is. A partner may read/create/edit
    // only their own account; sensitive fields the founder controls
    // (verification, badges, commission rate, payout holds, admin notes,
    // and the uid itself) can never be touched by the partner's own update.
    match /partners/{uid} {
      allow read: if isPartner(uid) || isAdmin();
      allow create: if isPartner(uid)
                    && request.resource.data.status == 'pending'
                    && !('commissionPct' in request.resource.data)
                    && !('verified' in request.resource.data)
                    && !('badges' in request.resource.data)
                    && !('payoutHold' in request.resource.data)
                    && !('adminNote' in request.resource.data)
                    && !('uid' in request.resource.data);
      allow update: if isAdmin()
                    || (isPartner(uid)
                        && !request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                             .hasAny(['status','verified','badges','commissionPct',
                                      'payoutHold','adminNote','uid']));
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

    // Room listings under a partner's own account. Public read (travelers
    // browse rooms without an account); only that partner or an admin may
    // manage the listings.
    //
    // SECURITY: checking exists(partners/{uid}) only proves the partner doc
    // exists, not that an admin has actually approved/verified the account -
    // an unverified (still 'pending') partner could already create/manage
    // room listings. Fixed by additionally requiring the partner doc's
    // verified field be true; admins still bypass this entirely.
    match /partners/{uid}/rooms/{roomId} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create, update, delete: if (isPartner(uid)
                                         && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/partners/$(uid))
                                         && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partners/$(uid)).data.verified == true)
                                        || isAdmin();
    }

    // ---- ROOM BOOKINGS --------------------------------------------------
    // Live-only addition, carried over as-is. Visible only to the two
    // parties on the booking (the partner and the guest) or an admin. Any
    // signed-in user may create a booking request; only the owning partner
    // may move it along (status/note/confirmation), never touching who the
    // parties are.
    match /roomBookings/{ref} {
      allow read: if isAdmin()
                  || (request.auth != null && resource.data.partnerUid == request.auth.uid)
                  || (request.auth != null && resource.data.guestUid == request.auth.uid);
      // SECURITY: exists(partners/{partnerUid}) only proves the partner doc
      // exists, not that an admin has approved/verified it - a guest could
      // book a still-'pending', unverified partner. Fixed by additionally
      // requiring that partner doc's verified field be true.
      allow create: if request.auth != null
                    && request.resource.data.status in ['requested','paid-unverified','confirmed-pay-later']
                    && request.resource.data.guestUid == request.auth.uid
                    && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/partners/$(request.resource.data.partnerUid))
                    && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/partners/$(request.resource.data.partnerUid)).data.verified == true;
      allow update: if isAdmin()
                    || (request.auth != null
                        && resource.data.partnerUid == request.auth.uid
                        && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                             .hasOnly(['status','partnerNote','confirmedAt']));
      allow delete: if isAdmin();
    }

  }
}
